{
  "raw": {
    "https://jobs.uca.edu/postings/19675": {
      "title": "Position Details",
      "description": "University of Central Arkansas Employment Opportunities | Research Compliance Officer Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs NEW   Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Research Compliance Officer Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Title Information Full-Time/Part-Time Full Time Salary Range 47,000 Will this position be Onsite, Remote, or Hybrid? Hybrid Position Details Position Information Posting Number NC1072026 Working Title Research Compliance Officer Department Org 441000 - Research Compliance Why should you work at UCA? We are excited that you are considering joining the UCA Family—the faculty, staff, students and alumni—who make the University of Central Arkansas a great place to work! At the University of Central Arkansas, we value our employees striving to maintain a work-life balance. We realize that this affects the overall well-being of employees while learning to prioritize challenging work and the ability to make a difference. Participation in university-wide flexible work schedules may be available for certain positions. Training is provided on systems or job-specific software, and tools are available supporting professional and personal development. The benefits package allows you to customize your benefits to meet your current needs, with the ability to adjust them as your priorities change. This includes university contributions to health, dental, life and disability insurance, 80% tuition waivers for employees and their families, 12 official holidays, immediate leave accrual, and a retirement program with university contributions of 10% of employee salary. Also available are health and wellness incentives, as well as access to sports and cultural activities on campus for the family, free gym membership and equipment rentals. Position Summary The RCO oversees all aspects of research compliance in the areas of human subject protection, animal care and use, financial conflicts of interest, export control, and the responsible conduct of research, and supports the Research Integrity Officer in matters involving allegations of noncompliance or research misconduct. The RCO serves as the Institutional Review Board administrator, the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee administrator, and the university’s Export Control Officer. The RCO establishes and maintains working relationships with individuals at all levels of the institution’s research community, educates the UCA community on the policies and procedures governing the review of research, and keeps current on the regulatory landscape through professional development, including regional and national conferences. Job Duties and Responsibilities Administers the human subjects protection program and serves as IRB administrator, managing protocol intake and review, committee support, continuing review, post-approval monitoring, and the institution’s federal assurances and reliance agreements. Administers the animal care and use program and serves as IACUC administrator, coordinating protocol review, semiannual program review and facility inspection, and the institution’s animal welfare assurance. Serves as Export Control Officer, conducting restricted party screening, evaluating the applicability of the fundamental research exclusion, and developing technology control plans as needed. Administers the financial conflict of interest program, including disclosure review, management plans, training verification, and required agency reporting. Supports the Research Integrity Officer in matters involving alleged noncompliance or research misconduct, including receiving allegations, sequestering and maintaining custody of research records, and providing administrative support to inquiry and investigation committees. The Research Integrity Officer retains decision-making authority. Develops and delivers compliance education, training, orientation, guidance documents, and individual consultation for faculty, staff, and students, and administers the university’s online research ethics training program. Administers the university’s electronic research compliance platform, maintains audit-ready records consistent with federal retention requirements, and prepares compliance reports for institutional leadership, the University Research Council, and external agencies. Reviews institutional research compliance policies and procedures and recommends revisions as federal and state regulations change, and serves as a resource during audits and federal site visits. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Working knowledge of the federal regulatory framework governing research, including human subjects protections, animal welfare, export control, financial conflicts of interest, and research misconduct. Ability to interpret and apply complex regulations accurately and consistently, and in a fair, reasonable, and service-oriented manner. Ability to exercise sound independent judgment, recognize the limits of that judgment, and escalate matters appropriately. Ability to manage multiple time-sensitive matters while maintaining strict confidentiality regarding sensitive personnel, research, and compliance information. Excellent written communication skills, including the ability to draft clear regulatory correspondence, policy language, and guidance materials. Strong interpersonal and presentation skills, with the ability to explain regulatory requirements to audiences with varying levels of research experience. Ability to establish and sustain productive working relationships across all levels of the institution, including faculty, department chairs, deans, general counsel, sponsored programs staff, and information technology. Knowledge of the organization and administration of higher education, including shared governance and the culture of academic research. Skills Considered Essential for Success -Knowledge of the organization and administration of higher education. -Knowledge of state, federal, and university rules, regulations and procedures. -Ability to work in a diverse and dynamic environment. -Ability to work collaboratively and independently with common goals as a part of a team. -Ability to make sound judgments. -Effective interpersonal communication, writing, and organizational abilities. -Civility in all interactions. -Demonstrate effective follow-up. Required Education and/or Experience Bachelor’s degree in psychology, a natural or social science, sociology, public administration, business, or a closely related field. A minimum of two years of professional experience in research administration, research compliance, IRB or IACUC support, regulatory affairs, or a substantially similar role. Demonstrated experience interpreting and applying regulations, policies, or procedures in a professional setting. Preferred Education and/or Experience Master’s degree or higher in a related field. Direct experience administering an IRB , IACUC , export control program, or financial conflict of interest program in a higher education or research setting. Experience conducting research involving human participants or laboratory animals. Certified IRB Professional ( CIP ) credential, or eligibility and willingness to obtain certification within two years of appointment. Experience with an electronic research compliance management system, such as Cayuse, IRBNet, or a comparable platform. Active participation in a relevant professional organization, such as PRIM &R, NCURA , SRA International, or the Association of University Export Control Officers. University, Campus, and Community Description Thank you for your interest in employment at the University of Central Arkansas. Founded in 1907, UCA has an enrollment of more than 10,000 students and employs over 1,300 full-time employees. The university offers more than 85 undergraduate and 59 graduate certificate and degree programs, including doctoral programs in five disciplines. UCA is proud of its tradition of teaching excellence and its contributions in research, scholarship, and creative activities. Desired start date 10/19/2026 Position Summary and Qualifications License(s)/Certifications Physical Demands Checklist Sitting, Standing, Lifting/Carrying, Walking Physical Exertion Administrative Work - Exerting up to 10 pounds, Primarily indoor work Posting Detail Information Background Check Statement This position may be subject to a pre-employment criminal, sex-offender registry, financial history background check and drug screening. A criminal conviction or arrest pending adjudication and/or adverse financial history alone shall not disqualify an applicant in the absence of a relationship to the requirements of the position. Background checks and drug screening information will be used in a confidential, non-discriminatory manner consistent with state and federal law. UCA EEO Statement The University of Central Arkansas is an equal opportunity institution. In keeping with its nondiscrimination policy in employment, admissions and other functions and programs, the university considers employees and students on the basis of individual merit and will not discriminate against a person on the basis of gender, race or color, ethnicity, religion, spiritual beliefs, national origin, age, familial status, socioeconomic background, sexual orientation, disability, political beliefs, intellectual perspective, genetic information, military status, or other factors irrelevant to participation in its programs. Open Date 08/25/2026 Close Date 09/18/2026 Open Until Filled No Special Instructions to Applicants Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Application Optional Documents Veteran's Documents License or Certification University of Central Arkansas Human Resources 201 Donaghey Ave. Wingo Hall Conway, AR 72035 1.501.450.3181 1.501.450.5088 (fax) humanresources@uca.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.uca.edu/postings/19418": {
      "title": "Position Details",
      "description": "University of Central Arkansas Employment Opportunities | CLASS Third Party Verification Field Observer Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs NEW   Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help CLASS Third Party Verification Field Observer Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please be prepared to share examples of observation notes from an observation previously conducted Posting Details Title Information Full-Time/Part-Time Full Time Salary Range $34,500 (July 1 - December 15, 2026) Will this position be Onsite, Remote, or Hybrid? Remote Position Details Position Information Posting Number NC0882026 Working Title CLASS Third Party Verification Field Observer Department Org 320000 - Dean-College of Education Why should you work at UCA? We are excited that you are considering joining the UCA Family—the faculty, staff, students and alumni—who make the University of Central Arkansas a great place to work! At the University of Central Arkansas, we value our employees striving to maintain a work-life balance. We realize that this affects the overall well-being of employees while learning to prioritize challenging work and the ability to make a difference. Participation in university-wide flexible work schedules may be available for certain positions. Training is provided on systems or job-specific software, and tools are available supporting professional and personal development. The benefits package allows you to customize your benefits to meet your current needs, with the ability to adjust them as your priorities change. This includes university contributions to health, dental, life and disability insurance, 80% tuition waivers for employees and their families, 12 official holidays, immediate leave accrual, and a retirement program with university contributions of 10% of employee salary. Also available are health and wellness incentives, as well as access to sports and cultural activities on campus for the family, free gym membership and equipment rentals. Position Summary The Arkansas Research Center ( ARC ) at the University of Central Arkansas is seeking twelve full-time Regional CLASS Observers to conduct independent observations as part of its statewide CLASS Third-Party Observation Verification project, conducted on behalf of the Arkansas Department of Education, Office of Early Childhood. ARC has been engaged to conduct verification observations across all 75 Arkansas counties. Observers are organized into six geographic service areas, with two observers assigned to each region. Candidates must indicate their preferred region at the time of application and must reside in that region. Positions vary in county coverage and travel demand: Northwest: Covers 3 counties — Benton, Carroll, and Washington. North/Ozarks: Covers 18 counties across the Ozark region, from Faulkner and Pope counties north through Baxter, Boone, and Newton. Northeast: Covers 10 counties across the northeastern Delta and river corridor, including Craighead, Crittenden, and Mississippi counties. Central: Covers Pulaski and Saline counties. West/River Valley: Covers 23 counties across western and south-central Arkansas. South/Delta: Covers 19 counties across the southern Delta, from Jefferson and Lonoke counties south through Ashley, Chicot, and Union. Qualified candidates will hold active Teachstone CLASS Observer certification for both the Infant-Toddler 2.0 and Pre-K 2.0 tools and bring a minimum of five years of experience in early childhood education or a closely related field, including at least three years of direct professional experience in 0–5 classroom settings. Candidates may not hold existing coaching, professional development, or supervisory relationships with early childhood programs in their preferred regional service area. This position is currently funded by the Arkansas Department of Education, Office of Early Childhood, for July 1 through December 15 due to federal funding limitations. ADE - OEC will determine additional funding in the fall; the full twelve-month salary would be $75,000. Job Duties and Responsibilities Conduct CLASS Infant-Toddler 2.0 and Pre-K 2.0 verification observations across all assigned counties within the regional service area, completing four full CLASS cycles per classroom in accordance with Teachstone protocol and ARC’s standardized observation procedures Contact program directors on the morning of each scheduled visit to confirm classroom availability; specific observation dates are not disclosed in advance to ensure observations reflect typical classroom practice Complete same-day data entry for all assigned observations, entering dimension-level scores and required documentation into ADE’s designated data system Submit observation scores on a biweekly basis consistent with ARC’s data reporting schedule Participate in ARC’s pre-deployment reliability training Participate in ongoing double-coding with the regional pair partner for a minimum of 10% of individual caseload per observation window, with structured debriefs to address scoring discrepancies Participate in shadow scoring sessions with local leads on a rotating basis to support cross-agency calibration Participate in monthly accuracy audits conducted by the Project Lead Disclose all professional relationships with early childhood programs and educators in the assigned regional service area prior to receiving observation assignments; notify the Project Manager immediately if a potential conflict of interest is identified Refrain from providing coaching, feedback, or professional development to educators or program staff in connection with or following verification visits Maintain all observation data and records in compliance with FERPA and Arkansas state data privacy standards Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge Comprehensive knowledge of the Classroom Assessment Scoring System® ( CLASS ), including the Infant-Toddler 2.0 and Pre-K 2.0 tools, Teachstone observation protocol, and inter-rater reliability standards Knowledge of infant-toddler and pre-K classroom environments, developmentally appropriate practice, and the range of program types serving Arkansas’s 0–5 population Knowledge of Arkansas’s early childhood program landscape, including licensed child care centers, family child care homes, Head Start/Early Head Start, and Arkansas Better Chance programs Knowledge of applicable federal and state data privacy requirements, including FERPA Skills Skill in conducting structured classroom observations with precision and consistency, including accurate cycle-level scoring across all CLASS dimensions for both the Infant-Toddler 2.0 and Pre-K 2.0 tools Skill in accurate same-day data entry using tablet-based systems in field conditions Strong organizational skills with the ability to manage an individual observation caseload, maintain documentation standards, and meet recurring reporting deadlines independently Strong interpersonal skills with the ability to interact professionally with program directors, educators, and administrators across a wide range of community and program settings Abilities Ability to conduct CLASS Infant-Toddler 2.0 and Pre-K 2.0 observations independently with a documented percent-within-one-level agreement rate of ≥90% Ability to work independently in a field-based, travel-intensive environment with minimal day-to-day supervision Ability to exercise sound professional judgment in identifying and disclosing potential conflicts of interest and maintaining the evaluative boundaries required of a third-party verifier Ability to manage extended travel demands, including overnight stays, across a multi-county regional service area Skills Considered Essential for Success -Knowledge of the organization and administration of higher education. -Knowledge of state, federal, and university rules, regulations and procedures. -Ability to work in a diverse and dynamic environment. -Ability to work collaboratively and independently with common goals as a part of a team. -Ability to make sound judgments. -Effective interpersonal communication, writing, and organizational abilities. -Civility in all interactions. -Demonstrate effective follow-up. Required Education and/or Experience Education Bachelor’s degree in early childhood education, child development, education, psychology, or a closely related field; or equivalent combination of education and professional experience Experience A minimum of five years of progressively responsible experience in early childhood education, child development, or a closely related field A minimum of three years of direct professional experience working in or closely with licensed 0–5 classroom settings, including infant-toddler and/or pre-K environments Demonstrated experience working with the full range of early childhood program types, including center-based child care, family child care homes, and/or school-based pre-K programs Other Requirements Must reside in the preferred regional service area Valid driver’s license and reliable personal vehicle required Must be willing and able to travel extensively within the assigned regional service area, including overnight stays as required by regional assignment No existing coaching, professional development, or supervisory relationships with early childhood programs or educators in the preferred regional service area, or demonstrated ability to be assigned exclusively to sites free of such conflicts Preferred Education and/or Experience Experience Prior experience conducting CLASS observations in a formal program evaluation, quality rating, or third-party verification context Experience with structured observational data collection, inter-rater reliability protocols, or program evaluation data management systems Familiarity with Arkansas’s early childhood system, including local lead organizations, Arkansas Better Chance, the Child Care Assistance Program, and QRIS participation requirements Bilingual proficiency in English and Spanish University, Campus, and Community Description Thank you for your interest in employment at the University of Central Arkansas. Founded in 1907, UCA has an enrollment of more than 10,000 students and employs over 1,300 full-time employees. The university offers more than 85 undergraduate and 59 graduate certificate and degree programs, including doctoral programs in five disciplines. UCA is proud of its tradition of teaching excellence and its contributions in research, scholarship, and creative activities. Desired start date 08/01/2026 Position Summary and Qualifications License(s)/Certifications Active Teachstone CLASS Observer certification for both the Infant-Toddler 2.0 and Pre-K 2.0 tools Physical Demands Checklist Physical Exertion Administrative Work - Exerting up to 10 pounds Posting Detail Information Background Check Statement This position may be subject to a pre-employment criminal, sex-offender registry, financial history background check and drug screening. A criminal conviction or arrest pending adjudication and/or adverse financial history alone shall not disqualify an applicant in the absence of a relationship to the requirements of the position. Background checks and drug screening information will be used in a confidential, non-discriminatory manner consistent with state and federal law. UCA EEO Statement The University of Central Arkansas is an equal opportunity institution. In keeping with its nondiscrimination policy in employment, admissions and other functions and programs, the university considers employees and students on the basis of individual merit and will not discriminate against a person on the basis of gender, race or color, ethnicity, religion, spiritual beliefs, national origin, age, familial status, socioeconomic background, sexual orientation, disability, political beliefs, intellectual perspective, genetic information, military status, or other factors irrelevant to participation in its programs. Open Date 07/14/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Special Instructions to Applicants Please be prepared to share examples of observation notes from an observation previously conducted Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Application License or Certification Optional Documents Veteran's Documents University of Central Arkansas Human Resources 201 Donaghey Ave. Wingo Hall Conway, AR 72035 1.501.450.3181 1.501.450.5088 (fax) humanresources@uca.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
